Pinned revision: 81aec25ccbb98f4873a255f7551ac4dadac59b4a
OPN/SSG/ADPCM core subset. See LICENSE.

Local patches:
1. Value-initialize fm_operator::m_cache. Normal synthesis fills it in prepare();
   initialization gives the fields a defined state and addresses cpp:S2107.
2. Add an optional host-owned YM2608 channel-output cache and an accessor to
   the last SSG outputs. When requested, FM/ADPCM voices are rendered individually
   before summing. The original mixed path remains available; patch 8 below
   removes the aggregate clamp from both paths.
   No clocks, envelopes, operator calculations or raw core state fields change.
   The native-stream wrapper clocks raw SSG separately; the old MAX-fidelity
   comparison path repeats SSG samples for differential tests.
3. Add explicit YM2608 port peeks, effective register accessors and safe SSG /
   ADPCM external-peek callbacks for debugger use. Normal read/write semantics
   and serialized core fields are unchanged. Hosts/SSG overrides without a
   peek callback return FFh rather than calling a potentially destructive read.
   Contrib/makoto-peek-test.cc checks values against cloned-core real reads and
   proves that peeks leave chip state and host side effects untouched.


4. Add a debugger register-write helper that uses normal port semantics but
   restores the CPU address latch afterward. The host synchronizes audio first.
   Contrib/makoto-register-test.py compares full chip state with equivalent I/O
   writes, including timers, ADPCM RAM and prescalers. No serialized fields change.

5. Fix inclusive ADPCM-B CPU transfer boundaries: store the last byte of
   the end chunk, and read the last byte of the limit chunk before wrapping.
   Confirmed with the Makoto V5 probe on Sanyo MSX2+ and Panasonic turbo R:
   x1 END=0 stores A1 A2 A3 A4; LIMIT=0 repeats B1 B2 B3 B4.
   Contrib/makoto-adpcm-transfer-test.cc covers these and software boundary
   regressions. Playback boundary helpers and serialized fields are unchanged.

6. Track an unfinished CPU RAM writer across mode changes. A CPU read before
   explicit ADPCM RESET returns the last written buffer byte, matching physical
   Makoto V4 rows A/B; reset permits normal reads (rows C/D). Peeks obey the same
   rule without side effects. The latch is appended as one serialized byte;
   no existing field or audio synthesis calculation is changed.
   This is a targeted write-to-read transition fix, not a complete bus-level
   model. The independent YM2608-LLE reference reproduced the stale-buffer
   transition; its code is not included or linked into this core.

Contrib/makoto-core-test.cc compares both paths, including chip state, for
360,000 samples covering all 16 voices and all three prescalers. It also pins a
1133-byte legacy prefix (FNV-1a 4faf7577) plus the new zero latch byte.
The current YM2608 state is 1134 bytes. This fingerprint is a regression
check, not a security checksum.

The initial upstream Makoto sound-state version 1 uses the 1134-byte layout.
Fork-only legacy migration remains in the fork. Future core changes must use
an explicit version/migration, not silently accept a matching byte count.

7. Rebuild the YM2608 derived sampling configuration after restoring its
   prescaler. This permits the wrapper to set fidelity only at construction.
   Contrib/makoto-prescale-restore-test.cc checks continuation at all three
   prescalers and fidelities; no serialized bytes change.

8. Leave the YM2608 FM/rhythm/ADPCM sum unclipped for openMSX's floating-point
   mixer. Separate voice buffers now use raw taps directly. Deliberately
   overdriven material differs from the old 16-bit internal clamp; final host
   output can still clip. Contrib/makoto-unclipped-mix-test.cc checks both
   ordinary levels and a coherent six-voice stress signal. No state fields change.
